{"id":"d.0a44cm","type":"DesignDecision","createdAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:03Z","date":"2026-09-23","decision":"HalfAndHalf keeps its everyday name.","metaRules":["naming-subclass-names-consistent"],"rationale":"That's what the carton says.","title":"Milk names stay natural","updatedAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:03Z"}
{"id":"d.8wg5vc","type":"DesignDecision","createdAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:02Z","date":"2026-09-23","decision":"The pure espresso drinks keep their menu names.","metaRules":["naming-subclass-names-consistent"],"rationale":"Nobody orders a LongBlackEspresso.","title":"Menu names for pure espressos","updatedAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:02Z"}
{"id":"d.386rne","type":"DesignDecision","createdAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:01Z","date":"2026-09-23","decision":"Espresso, Ristretto, Lungo and the other drinks keep the names a menu uses.","metaRules":["naming-subclass-names-consistent"],"rationale":"Baristas say Lungo, not LungoEspresso. The parent names group them, so the children don't repeat a word.","title":"Drinks keep their own names","updatedAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:01Z"}
{"id":"d.h6nzmn","type":"DesignDecision","createdAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:00Z","date":"2026-09-23","decision":"Build from scratch.","rationale":"No coffee vocabulary is small enough to teach with, so this ontology starts from scratch. A real café would look at a food or beverage vocabulary first.","title":"No existing ontology reused","updatedAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:00Z"}
{"id":"d.jctqnh","type":"DesignDecision","createdAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:04Z","date":"2026-09-23","decision":"Every drink type, Cortado included, is a class. Items on the menu are instances.","rationale":"The questions compare kinds of drinks, and each kind has its own facts. A café that only listed menu items would model them as instances.","title":"Cortado is a class","updatedAt":"2026-09-23T00:00:04Z"}
